> How should I mention a non-peer-reviewed chapter in a book on my academic resume? I have a "Peer-Reviewed Publications" section and a "Reports and Online Books" section. I think it should go in the latter, but maybe I need to change the title of that section.
**assistant**:
> It's quite common to include non-peer-reviewed book chapters under a separate section from peer-reviewed articles. You might consider renaming your "Reports and Online Books" section to something broader like "Chapters, Reports, and Online Books" or "Other Publications" to better capture the nature of the content.
> 
> Here's how you might structure your entry:
> 
> **Chapters, Reports, and Online Books**
> 
> - Author(s). "Chapter Title." In *Book Title*, edited by Editor(s), Page Range. Publisher, Year. [Additional details, like if it was part of a significant institution's report or involved noteworthy collaborations.]
> 
> This structure ensures clarity and gives due credit to the publication while distinguishing it from peer-reviewed work. By broadening the section title, you also create flexibility for including a variety of publication types that don't fit neatly into the peer-reviewed category.
